INT. FEED STORE BACK ROOM - NIGHT

Grain sacks stacked to the rafters. A single oil lamp. Barn-cat eyes in the dark, gone when you look.

SHERIFF CASIMIR DUVALL (50s, silver mustache, a limp from an old stirrup) sits at a plank table, chair tilted back. Opposite him, TOBIAS "TOBY" RAINEY (20s, barely a beard, hands cuffed in front, a bruise blooming on his jaw).

In the corner, behind the sacks, a woman stands motionless. MABEL KINGSLEY (40s, widow's black, a shotgun held low like a broom).

                    DUVALL
          You know whose barn that was.

                    TOBY
          I know.

                    DUVALL
          You know whose horse.

                    TOBY
          I know that too.

                    DUVALL
          You know the penalty.

                    TOBY
          Sheriff, I'll hang if that's what it is. I'm not asking you to like me.

                    DUVALL
          I don't.

                    TOBY
          Then we agree.

                    MABEL
          Ask him where the boy is.

                    DUVALL
          Mabel.

                    MABEL
          Ask him.

                    DUVALL
          Where's the boy, Toby?

                    TOBY
          I left him at the creek. He's fine. He's twelve and he's fine.

                    MABEL
          Alone.

                    TOBY
          With a canteen and a blanket, ma'am. Which is more than I got at twelve.

                    DUVALL
          Why the creek?

                    TOBY
          Because the barn was on fire, and I'd have had to carry him.

                    MABEL
          You set the fire.

                    TOBY
          I did not.

                    DUVALL
          Then who?

                    TOBY
          Ask the man who held the lamp. Ask the one who paid the stable hand two dollars to go quiet.

Duvall lets his chair drop forward. The legs hit the floor.

                    DUVALL
          Name him.

Toby looks at Mabel. At the shotgun. At Duvall.

                    TOBY
          You'll want to sit down, ma'am.
